WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.144
Designing E-Learning: Multimedia, UDL, and Accessibility.

2
00:00:06.144 --> 00:00:10.455
ID 553 - Module 6: Creating Instruction.

3
00:00:10.455 --> 00:00:16.922
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:17.522 --> 00:00:23.946
Richard Mayer's cognitive theory of multimedia learning
rests on three assumptions.

5
00:00:23.946 --> 00:00:28.203
People process words and pictures in separate channels.

6
00:00:28.203 --> 00:00:30.835
Each channel has limited capacity.

7
00:00:30.835 --> 00:00:37.027
And learning requires active processing: selecting,
organizing, and integrating.

8
00:00:37.027 --> 00:00:43.761
From decades of experiments, Mayer derived principles for
combining words and pictures.

9
00:00:43.761 --> 00:00:51.269
They reduce extraneous load and direct attention, and they
apply with the most force for novices.

10
00:00:51.269 --> 00:00:56.300
Experts can tolerate, and sometimes prefer, denser
presentations.

11
00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:01.584
Picture a screen from an e-learning module on barcode
scanning.

12
00:01:01.584 --> 00:01:12.363
There's a paragraph of text, a stock photo of a smiling
nurse, and a small workflow diagram in the corner with its
labels in a legend underneath.

13
00:01:12.363 --> 00:01:18.831
The narration reads the paragraph word for word, and
background music plays throughout.

14
00:01:18.831 --> 00:01:20.095
Three violations.

15
00:01:20.095 --> 00:01:26.637
Redundancy: identical words as narration and on-screen text
overload the verbal channel.

16
00:01:26.637 --> 00:01:30.800
Coherence: the stock photo and the music are extraneous.

17
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:39.572
Spatial contiguity: the labels sit in a legend instead of on
the diagram, so the learner's eyes bounce back and forth.

18
00:01:40.172 --> 00:01:42.471
Now the same screen redesigned.

19
00:01:42.471 --> 00:01:48.032
The diagram fills the screen, and each label sits on the
step it describes.

20
00:01:48.032 --> 00:02:01.008
The narration explains the diagram rather than reading a
paragraph, and the on-screen text is reduced to three key
terms highlighted at the moment the narration mentions them.

21
00:02:01.008 --> 00:02:03.455
The photo and the music are gone.

22
00:02:03.455 --> 00:02:08.794
A caption toggle provides the narration as text for anyone
who needs it.

23
00:02:08.794 --> 00:02:15.615
Five principles applied: redundancy, coherence, spatial
contiguity, signaling, and modality.

24
00:02:15.615 --> 00:02:23.030
Nothing was added; the design got better by removing things
and putting the rest in the right place.

25
00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:26.439
A few more principles worth memorizing.

26
00:02:26.439 --> 00:02:35.368
Temporal contiguity: present corresponding words and
pictures at the same time, so narration is synchronized with
animation.

27
00:02:35.368 --> 00:02:42.066
Segmenting: present learner-paced chunks with a continue
control rather than one long stream.

28
00:02:42.066 --> 00:02:48.907
Pre-training: teach the names and characteristics of key
components before showing the process.

29
00:02:48.907 --> 00:02:52.075
Personalization: use a conversational style.

30
00:02:52.075 --> 00:02:58.197
And the voice principle: people learn better from a human
voice than a machine voice.

31
00:02:58.197 --> 00:03:03.021
You may notice the narration you're hearing right now is
synthetic.

32
00:03:03.021 --> 00:03:10.727
That's deliberate for a draft, and it's exactly the kind of
thing you'd test with learners before shipping.

33
00:03:11.327 --> 00:03:25.941
Universal Design for Learning, from CAST, is a framework for
designing instruction that works for the widest range of
learners from the start, instead of retrofitting
accommodations afterward.

34
00:03:25.941 --> 00:03:27.235
Three principles.

35
00:03:27.235 --> 00:03:36.369
Multiple means of engagement, the why: choice within a task,
relevance made explicit, clear goals and progress feedback.

36
00:03:36.369 --> 00:03:46.036
Multiple means of representation, the what: captions,
transcripts, text alternatives for images, glossaries,
multiple examples.

37
00:03:46.036 --> 00:03:58.366
Multiple means of action and expression, the how:
alternatives to mouse-only interaction, choice of product
format where the objective allows, planning templates.

38
00:03:58.366 --> 00:04:01.944
UDL is not 'make three versions of everything'.

39
00:04:01.944 --> 00:04:10.697
It's a set of checkpoints to consult during design so the
most common barriers are removed before anyone hits them.

40
00:04:11.297 --> 00:04:20.003
The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines define what makes
digital content usable by people with disabilities.

41
00:04:20.003 --> 00:04:31.638
Version 2.2 is current, and level double-A conformance is
the standard in most institutional policies and in United
States law for public entities.

42
00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:32.904
Four principles.

43
00:04:32.904 --> 00:04:43.589
Perceivable: text alternatives for images, captions and
transcripts, sufficient color contrast, no information
conveyed by color alone.

44
00:04:43.589 --> 00:04:52.770
Operable: everything works with a keyboard, focus is
visible, nothing flashes dangerously, navigation is
consistent.

45
00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:59.893
Understandable: the language is identified, behavior is
predictable, errors are explained.

46
00:04:59.893 --> 00:05:10.182
Robust: use real headings, lists, and form labels rather
than visual imitations, so assistive technologies can read
the structure.

47
00:05:10.782 --> 00:05:13.465
Here's the checklist for every asset.

48
00:05:13.465 --> 00:05:18.831
Meaningful images have alt text and decorative ones are
marked decorative.

49
00:05:18.831 --> 00:05:22.892
Videos have accurate captions and audio has transcripts.

50
00:05:22.892 --> 00:05:26.083
Headings are real headings in logical order.

51
00:05:26.083 --> 00:05:30.434
Contrast passes and links are distinguishable without color.

52
00:05:30.434 --> 00:05:38.193
Every interaction works with the keyboard alone, and drag-
and-drop or timed interactions have alternatives.

53
00:05:38.193 --> 00:05:41.964
Documents pass their built-in accessibility checker.

54
00:05:41.964 --> 00:05:51.028
If you run a screen reader over our before screen, it reads
a paragraph, then the word 'image', then a jumble of legend
text.

55
00:05:51.028 --> 00:05:59.223
Over the after screen, it reads the heading, the diagram's
alt text describing the workflow, and three key terms.

56
00:05:59.223 --> 00:06:12.131
Accessibility and Mayer's principles reinforce each other
far more often than they conflict, and where they seem to,
provide both: narration with captions the learner can
toggle.

57
00:06:12.731 --> 00:06:16.710
Tool choice follows strategy and media decisions.

58
00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:26.212
Storyline is powerful and expensive; Rise is fast and
constrained; H5P is free and good enough for most
interactions.

59
00:06:26.212 --> 00:06:29.217
Camtasia, OBS, and Loom handle video.

60
00:06:29.217 --> 00:06:31.409
Twine prototypes branching.

61
00:06:31.409 --> 00:06:42.292
Generative AI can draft scripts, alt text, scenario
variants, and, as you've heard, narration; verify everything
and disclose its use.

62
00:06:42.292 --> 00:06:52.037
Modules reach an LMS as SCORM packages or xAPI statements,
and whole courses travel between systems as Common
Cartridge.

63
00:06:52.037 --> 00:07:06.250
For your instructional plan, list every media asset in the
lesson you storyboard, name the Mayer principles that govern
it, and note the accessibility requirements that apply.

64
00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:09.742
That list becomes part of assignment seven.
